WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.220
Paleontology.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.100
I know what you're all thinking.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:09.900
"Jurassic Park."

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.660
Maybe Ross from "Friends."

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.020
(Laughter)

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.140
Perhaps even Indiana Jones?

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.860
But that's archaeology.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.540
Let me show you some real paleontology.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.540
Now, first of all,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.780
let's take a step back in time.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.420
Not quite to the Jurassic,
at least not yet,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.580
but to January 21, 2021,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.980
when I received an email

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.260
about a curious discovery made
in Rutland in the Midlands.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.700
As a paleontologist,
you can probably imagine,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.340
I get countless messages and emails
from people saying,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.700
"Oh, we've made a cool fossil discovery."

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.860
Being completely honest, most of the time,

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.020
it turns out to be, well,
a weird shaped rock,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.140
(Laughter)

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.220
But, little did I know this email
would change my life.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.700
The find had been made Rutland Water
Nature Reserve by Joe Davis,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.660
who works for the Leicestershire
and Rutland Wildlife Trust

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.100
who run the reserve in partnership
with Anglian Water, who own the land.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.500
Whilst doing some routine maintenance
in one of the Reserve's lagoons,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.420
he'd lowered the water levels

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.780
and Joe had spotted
by chance this structure.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.900
Unsure of what to make of it, he thought,

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.380
"Could this be a bit of old pipe, maybe?

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.620
Or perhaps,

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.220
is this a dinosaur skeleton?” he joked.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.220
Luckily,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.540
Joe thought to himself, "Well,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.380
could it be an important discovery?"

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.580
So crucially, he sought expert advice.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.940
When these photos pinged into my inbox,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.380
I immediately recognized them
as large vertebrae

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.220
from the spine of an ichthyosaur,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.980
or as it’s colloquially known
as a sea dragon.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.540
Now, I do need to clear
that name up just a little bit,

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.380
because, I'm sorry to say,
they're not dragons.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.660
Sorry.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.620
They're also not swimming dinosaurs.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.700
They’re not the Loch Ness
Monster on tour either.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.260
And they're most definitely not,
as the BBC dubbed them

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.580
"Sea dragon dinosaur dolphin fossils."

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.540
(Laughter)

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:36.820
Terrible.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.620
Ichthyosaurs are in fact

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.660
an awesome group
of ancient marine reptiles

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.500
that lived in the prehistoric seas
as dinosaurs reigned supreme on the land.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.780
Like modern-day whales and dolphins,

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.780
they gave birth to live young,
and they breathed air.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.380
Unfortunately, ichthyosaurs
went extinct around 90 million years ago.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.460
Our Rutland ichthyosaur lived
during the early Jurassic,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.220
around about 180 million years ago.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.660
This is a time when Rutland
was deep underwater,

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.340
covered by an ancient tropical sea.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.460
Now I spent most of my professional career

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.300
of around 14 years

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.580
studying thousands of ichthyosaurs.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.700
And this has been a great pleasure
to travel around the world,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.660
study lots and lots of ichthyosaurs.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.140
And so when I received these photos,

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.980
I immediately knew that this could be
something very special indeed.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.140
But of course, just looking at photos,

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.740
it's hard to know exactly whether
this is going to be something unique

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.420
or is it just a few vertebrae?

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.380
After speaking to fellow marine
reptile expert Dr. Mark Evans,

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.740
who was also sent details of the find,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.380
we decided to visit the site in February.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.540
And I led a mini excavation,

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.300
a bit of an exploratory dig
to see what we could uncover.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.540
On a cold day in February,

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.060
where the ground was literally frozen
right beneath our feet,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.100
after spending so much time
meticulously removing the Jurassic clay

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.820
from around the skeleton,

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.020
we were blown away

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:23.900
because we revealed what appeared to be
a gigantic skeleton

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.300
unlike anything ever found
in Britain before.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.900
Clearly, this was a big deal.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.660
But we had to be very quick
to contain our excitement.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.100
Because due to the damp wintery conditions

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.380
and the fact that this
was a super fragile skeleton,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.580
it meant it wasn't the right time
to collect this ichthyosaur.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.180
So unfortunately, might seem
a little bit counterintuitive,

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.900
but we had to rebury the ichthyosaur.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.900
And we had to cover it with plastic sheets
and then Jurassic clay.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.820
Because you have to remember, this,
of course, is an active nature reserve.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.580
And so the nature reserve,

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.660
they had to raise the water levels.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.340
Then the ichthyosaur
went back under the water,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.180
but it was actually protected.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.740
And then waiting for what felt
like millions of years.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.660
(Laughter)

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.220
In six months we were back on site,

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.700
but this time with a superb
team of paleontologists.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.740
When we arrived on site,
we had to pick up where we left off.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.780
And so we removed those plastic sheets,
remove the Jurassic clay,

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.380
and start to expose
some more of the skeleton.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.860
We were overly ambitious in February.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:44.100
We thought, based
on that little dig that we did,

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.300
the one-day excavation,

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:48.900
we thought, OK, we could probably
get the entire skeleton out of the ground

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:50.100
in just one week.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.620
Yeah, we were wrong.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.020
The skeleton was much larger,
more important and more complete

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.100
than any of us would have imagined.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.900
And it doubled our time on site.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:06.500
We spent 14 and a half days altogether,
spread over three weeks,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:12.380
and this was also working very long hours,
from sunrise to sunset.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:16.340
Whilst the team was excavating
the ichthyosaur,

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.500
we also had to look out
for all sorts of other pieces

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.460
in the surrounding matrix
of the ichthyosaur.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:23.660
So in my hand here,

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.980
I'm actually holding one of the isolated
teeth from this ichthyosaur.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:31.140
We also found hundreds
of these squid-like ammonites,

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.820
we found lots of fossil shells,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.460
and these are all really important

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.820
because they help to build up
this bigger picture

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:40.700
of the ancient Jurassic ecosystem
in which this ichthyosaur lived.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.220
The team also had to move fast as well.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:48.460
This really was quite a race against time,
because once we exposed the skeleton,

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:51.380
there was more chance that it could
end up degrading further.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.340
So team members like Dr. Emma Nicholls
here, had to apply a consolidant,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.620
a thin glue that seeps into the bones

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.620
and helps them to become more robust
to remove the skeleton out of the ground.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.820
Once we fully exposed the skeleton,

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:10.820
we then could eventually record all of our
measurements and get a full data set.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.860
For example, like for the skull.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:17.980
Nigel here, who I co-led
the excavation with,

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:20.860
so Nigel focused on
the conservation side of things,

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.380
ensuring that the ichthyosaur
remains safe and secure

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.100
throughout the entire excavation,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:26.940
Nigel is holding

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.460
an ichthyosaur, a regular-size
ichthyosaur skull.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.020
Look at the size comparison

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.700
to this Jurassic giant
that we're excavating.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.060
Pretty phenomenal.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.780
And this ichthyosaur,
I should say, that Nigel's holding,

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.820
this is actually of a species
I named after him a few years ago,

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.620
called Ichthyosaurs larkini.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:47.100
Whilst on site,

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:48.860
one of the team members, Steve Dey,

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.300
created a wonderful photogrammetry model,

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.660
which, photogrammetry
is an interesting technique

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.420
where you take thousands of photos

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.180
and put them together and build
a three-dimensional model.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:02.020
Now what you're looking at
first of all here,

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.740
is a base model that we made
back in February.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:06.540
But watch this little video.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:07.740
As we begin to zoom in,

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.580
you can see some of the spine
that we exposed in February.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:13.620
It's pretty cool, it's three-dimensional.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.940
But now watch, you can superimpose
the entire skeleton

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.660
that we'd revealed in the summer.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:20.980
Here's the main excavation.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:22.740
Now we can zoom in

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.420
and you can look at all
of the finer details of the bones

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.260
and the three-dimensionality of them.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.700
And like magic, now we remove
the Jurassic clay.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:31.180
Now we zoom out

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.580
and are able to see just
how complete this ichthyosaur is.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.060
So you start from the very
tip of the snout

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:40.060
all the way down
to the penny-sized vertebrae.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:41.260
Pretty cool.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:44.420
And we're even able
to pick out individual bones

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.860
and look at them in three
dimensions like this.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.340
This is the sort of techniques

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.580
that are sort of
revolutionizing paleontology.

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:54.540
We're able to capture all of this data
whilst the ichthyosaur sat in the ground.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.420
So before we've even removed anything.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.340
Now we've taken that out of the ground,

00:08:58.340 --> 00:08:59.500
we still have this data,

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.420
and we can send that to our colleagues
all around the world.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:05.940
Of course, now fully uncovered --

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.100
it’s me taking a nap --

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:13.580
It's time to literally get this Jurassic
giant out of the ground.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.380
Much easier said than done

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.340
when you're dealing with such a complete

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:21.100
and very heavy fossil
weighing several tons.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.140
The first port of call

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:27.100
was to create a trench
all the way around the skeleton.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:31.300
And the reason for that is that we needed
to get right on underneath the skeleton

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.980
so that we could begin the process
of plaster jacketing.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.900
So those white bits you can see
in the image, that's not snow,

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.660
although it was very cold
a few days on site,

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.180
that's actually plaster.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:45.380
Plaster jacketing is an important process
in the field of paleontology.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.020
We use these protective plaster jackets

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:53.060
that essentially care for and secure
the bones and the surrounding matrix

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.940
so that we can take them
out of the field and into the lab

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.820
so that we can analyze
the fossil and the matrix

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.380
and work out what's going on.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.980
We also had to, for the bigger
skull block and the body block,

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:08.180
we also had to create a wooden frame
to help support the immense weight.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:14.020
After various challenges on site,

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.540
we finally managed to remove
the entire skeleton

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.580
after dissecting it into several
more manageable blocks.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:23.060
But this one block here,
which is the main body block,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.980
this weighed one and a half tons.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.340
It's actually being removed,
which is quite nice,

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.700
it's actually being removed by Joe Davis,
who found the ichthyosaur.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.940
And this was an interesting
moment for the team

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:35.900
because it's quite an emotional moment,

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.500
because we'd spent and invested
so much time and energy

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:40.140
into this ichthyosaur excavation

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.500
that this moment captured that final piece

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.100
where we're removing this
from its final resting place,

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.060
180 million years ago.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.940
On January 10, 2022,

00:10:54.980 --> 00:11:00.660
this is almost one year after Joe
had made that incredible discovery,

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:03.500
Angling Water and the University
of Manchester

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.380
issued a joint press release
announcing the find.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.980
Here’s Joe and I on the BBC Breakfast
Studio chatting about this discovery

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.100
and revealing it to the world.

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.380
Amazingly, this story went viral.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:20.020
We were picked up on a multitude
of different platforms,

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:24.380
TV, radio, newspaper,
it went viral all across social media.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.820
We were thrilled to bits
with this, it was amazing,

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:29.500
amazing response
about our beautiful ichthyosaur.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.060
But one of the things is,

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.940
is why do you think exactly
did this ichthyosaur get so much interest?

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.260
First of all,

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.340
everybody loves a cool fossil,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.220
especially when it's big
and it's complete.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:45.020
So you've got a massive tick there.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:49.820
But genuinely, this ichthyosaur
was an apex predator

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:53.660
at the very top of its food chain
in the Jurassic sea.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:55.140
So again, another big tick.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:58.260
And admittedly
the photograph is very cool,

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.380
although it is me again laying down.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:01.940
(Laughter)

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:04.660
But on top of that, one thing
it's important to highlight

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:08.820
is the very fact that Britain
is the birthplace of ichthyosaurs.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.740
This ichthyosaur specimen in the photo,

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.580
this was the first ichthyosaur specimen

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.340
brought to the attention of science,

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:21.860
found by Mary and Joseph Anning
more than 200 years ago

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:24.700
in the Lyme Regis on the Dorset coast.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:30.980
Nowadays, finding ichthyosaur
fossils in the UK,

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.100
generally they're found on the Somerset,

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:36.060
the Dorset and the Yorkshire coastlines.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.740
And that's because you have the constant
erosion of the foreshore and the cliffs.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:42.460
You might be lucky to find, say,

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:46.180
the isolated one or two vertebrae
on the beach if you go looking.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:48.660
But still finding a complete skeleton,

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:52.300
and especially anything large
on the coastline is incredibly rare.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:56.020
This is why finding the Rutland
ichthyosaur in the center of the UK

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:59.740
in an unusual exposure
is so extraordinarily rare.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:05.620
So what next for this
significant specimen?

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.100
Well, despite ongoing plans

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:12.820
to try to replace the Rutland Water
Nature Reserve with “Joerassic Park” --

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:14.420
(Laughter)

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:15.580
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:16.780
I apologize.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:20.100
Compose myself.

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:22.980
No, the next major step

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:28.500
is for Nigel to clean and conserve
the entire skeleton.

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.180
And this is going to take
at least two years

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.780
to get to that point.

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:34.820
At that stage, then,

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:37.740
me and the rest of the team
are able to study the ichthyosaur

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:39.460
in as much detail as possible

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:44.780
and then prepare it
for its eventual display at Rutland,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.620
where it will then attract,

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:49.340
hopefully millions of people
will travel around the world

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:52.220
to come and see this specimen,
marvel at this Jurassic giant,

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.100
and understand the hidden history
right beneath their feet.

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:00.180
What an incredible journey.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:05.060
With most fossil finds,

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.780
there's always an element
of luck involved.

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:11.300
You know, being in the right place
at the right time.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:15.940
People search their entire lives
for something like this

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:17.700
and never even come close.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.860
The funny thing is, Joe wasn't
even looking for this.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:25.820
He genuinely stumbled upon
this exceptional discovery.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.100
At ten meters long,

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:33.220
the Rutland ichthyosaur
is the most complete skeleton

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:36.980
of a large prehistoric reptile
ever found in the UK.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.380
This once-in-a-lifetime discovery

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:44.420
represents one of the greatest finds
in British paleontological history.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.300
On a personal level,

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:48.980
leading this excavation

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.300
and revealing this Jurassic giant has been
one of the highlights of my career.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:56.860
Thank you all very much for letting me
share this journey with you.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:59.260
(Applause)

